-- allium: 3
-- schedules.allium
--
-- Scope: Scheduled tasks. A task can carry a cron schedule; when its next due
--        time arrives, a running board dispatches it. A phoenix task repeats
--        on its schedule; any other task runs once.
-- Includes: The schedule fields on Task and how they are set and cleared;
--           when a task is due; which board fires it; what a fire, a
--           successful launch and a failed launch do to the schedule; how a
--           phoenix successor inherits it; the epic chain passing over a
--           waiting task.
-- Excludes: How the agent turns "every week" into a cron string (the agent's
--           own judgement; the tool only takes the cron string); the dispatch
--           request lifecycle itself (store-split.allium: RequestDispatch and
--           the M4 rules); provisioning (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch);
--           the card and detail rendering (board-visuals.allium: "Schedule
--           marker"); the MCP argument contracts (mcp-task-tools.allium).
-- Dependencies: Task, Host and local_host() from core.allium;
--               DispatchRequest and DispatchRequestOrigin from
--               store-split.allium; PhoenixRespawn from tasks.allium.
--
-- Decisions taken with the user (task #98394), recorded so they are not
-- re-litigated:
--   - S1, a fire DISPATCHES. When a schedule comes due the task gets an agent,
--     through the same dispatch request every other launch path writes
--     (origin = schedule). Nothing else happens at the due time.
--   - S2, the schedule is a cron string. Standard five fields (minute, hour,
--     day of month, month, day of week), minute precision, read in UTC. A
--     caller who means "next Monday" or "every week" has the agent convert
--     the words into a cron string; the store never parses words.
--   - S3, phoenix decides recurrence. There is no run-once flag. A phoenix
--     task with a schedule repeats: each run's done respawns a successor
--     (tasks.allium: PhoenixRespawn) that carries the schedule and its own
--     next due time. Any other task with a schedule fires once.
--   - S4, missed runs collapse to one. A task whose due time passed while no
--     board was running fires once, as soon as a board runs. The runs it
--     missed are not made up: the next due time is computed from the moment
--     the successor is created, not from the missed slot.
--   - S5, overlap is skipped by construction. While a phoenix run is still
--     going (running or review) there is no backlog copy to fire, so a slot
--     that passes meanwhile is skipped. No separate overlap check exists.
--   - S6, any running board fires a due task, but only a board whose machine
--     holds the task's repo. A board without the repo leaves the task for one
--     that has it, so a missing repo never marks a schedule failed.
--     Deliberately ANY person's board, not only the setter's: a shared board
--     is shared between teammates, so a scheduled task in a shared epic is
--     work any of them may run. A task that must not run on a teammate's
--     machine belongs on its owner's own board or a private epic, where no
--     one else's board sees it. Raised by a security review and confirmed
--     with the user; not to be re-litigated.
--   - S7, a failed scheduled launch is marked, not retried. The task stays in
--     backlog with the failure reason. A one-off stops there; a phoenix task
--     moves on to its next due time.
--   - S8, the epic chain passes over a task still waiting for its time
--     (epics.allium: AutoDispatchNextSubtask).
--   - S9, the TUI shows a schedule and never edits it. Setting and clearing
--     are MCP-only (create_task and update_tasks' `schedule` argument).

use "./core.allium" as core
use "./store-split.allium" as store_split

config {
    -- How long a board waits after asking to dispatch a due task before it
    -- may ask again for the same task (FireDueTask).
    schedule_refire_after: Duration = 30.seconds
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The cron grammar the store accepts. Five whitespace-separated fields:
-- minute (0-59), hour (0-23), day of month (1-31), month (1-12), day of week
-- (0-6, 0 = Sunday; 7 also means Sunday). Each field takes `*`, a number, a
-- range `a-b`, a list `a,b,c` and a step `*/n` or `a-b/n`; month and weekday
-- also take their three-letter English names. Nothing else: no seconds field,
-- no year field, no `@weekly` macros, no `L`/`W`/`#`.
--
-- next_match(cron, after) is the first whole UTC minute STRICTLY after
-- `after` that the cron matches. Day of month and day of week combine the
-- standard way: when both are restricted (neither field starts with `*`, so
-- `*/2` counts as unrestricted, as in Vixie cron), a day matches if EITHER
-- does. A cron that matches no minute at all (e.g. "0 0 31 2 *") is
-- refused when it is set, so next_match always has an answer for a stored
-- schedule, searched from any instant: a cron accepted today keeps
-- recurring. The store searches a bounded window ahead, longer than the
-- longest gap between two matches of any cron the grammar accepts. That gap
-- is measured, not derived: over every (month, day of month, weekday) triple
-- in the 400-year Gregorian cycle it is 14609 days (about 40 years). E.g.
-- "0 0 29 2 */7" (29 February when it is a Sunday; AND, as the weekday field
-- starts with `*`) matches in 2088 and next in 2128, across the leap day 2100
-- skips.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Setting and clearing
------------------------------------------------------------

rule SetSchedule {
    when: ScheduleSet(task, cron)

    requires: cron_is_valid(cron)
    -- A non-phoenix task fires once, from backlog. Set on one that has left
    -- backlog it could never fire, so it is refused rather than stored inert.
    -- A phoenix task may take a schedule in any status: the run in progress
    -- is not affected, and the successor its done respawns carries it.
    requires: task.status = backlog or task.phoenix

    ensures: task.schedule = cron
    ensures: task.schedule_error = null
    ensures: task.next_due =
        if task.status = backlog: next_match(cron, after: now) else: null

    @guidance
        -- Reached from create_task's and update_tasks' `schedule` argument
        -- (mcp-task-tools.allium: CreateTaskViaMcp, UpdateTaskViaMcp), and
        -- applied by the store in the same write as the rest of the patch, so
        -- a task is never seen half-scheduled. Re-setting the same cron
        -- recomputes next_due from now and clears a recorded failure: setting
        -- a schedule is how a caller re-arms a one-off that failed.
        -- The refusals read "invalid cron schedule \"<cron>\": <why>" and
        -- "a schedule on a non-phoenix task needs it in backlog", and write
        -- nothing.
}

rule ClearSchedule {
    when: ScheduleCleared(task)

    ensures: task.schedule = null
    ensures: task.next_due = null
    ensures: task.schedule_error = null

    @guidance
        -- update_tasks with `schedule` set to the empty string. Clearing a
        -- task with no schedule is a no-op, not a refusal.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Firing
------------------------------------------------------------

rule FireDueTask {
    when: task: core/Task.next_due <= now

    requires: task.status = backlog
    -- S6: only a board whose machine holds the repo fires. Another board
    -- leaves the task due for one that does.
    requires: task.repo_path != "" and directory_exists_locally(task.repo_path)
    requires: not store_split/DispatchRequests.any(r => r.task = task and r.is_open)

    ensures: store_split/DispatchRequested(
        task: task,
        requester: core/local_host(),
        origin: schedule,
        mode: null
    )

    @guidance
        -- HOST-SIDE. Every running board checks the tasks it can see on its
        -- periodic tick and requests a dispatch for each due one; the board's
        -- clock only COMPARES against the stored next_due, it never computes
        -- one. The first board to tick after a start fires every task whose
        -- time passed while no board ran (S4). Two boards that both see the
        -- task due race on the request; the store accepts one
        -- (OneOpenDispatchRequestPerTask, and the claim moves the task out of
        -- backlog) and refuses the other, which is an ordinary refusal the
        -- board ignores. A refusal for any other reason is logged, and so is a
        -- task skipped for its repo. Either way the board asks again for the
        -- same task only after config.schedule_refire_after, and only while
        -- it is still due: the gap covers the round trip in which the claim
        -- moves the task out of backlog, so the ticks in between repeat no
        -- request.
        -- mode is left to RequestDispatch's own derivation (D3).
}

rule LeavingBacklogCancelsThePendingRun {
    -- Every status write other than the dispatch claim. The claim moves the
    -- task to running and keeps next_due until the launch ends
    -- (DispatchConsumesTheDueTime, ScheduledLaunchFails).
    when: TaskStatusWrittenByHand(task, from, to)

    requires: from = backlog and to != backlog

    ensures: task.next_due = null

    @guidance
        -- A hand move (the TUI's moves and editor, update_tasks' status)
        -- cancels the pending run; a close cancels it from any status
        -- (ClosingCancelsThePendingRun). A one-off is then spent:
        -- moving it back to backlog does not fire it; setting its schedule
        -- again re-arms it. A phoenix task keeps its schedule: the copy its
        -- done respawns is armed as usual, and a hand move back to backlog
        -- re-arms it (EnteringBacklogRearmsAPhoenixTask). task.schedule and
        -- task.schedule_error are kept as a record.
}

rule EnteringBacklogRearmsAPhoenixTask {
    -- The same writes as LeavingBacklogCancelsThePendingRun, the other way.
    when: TaskStatusWrittenByHand(task, from, to)

    requires: from != backlog and to = backlog
    requires: task.phoenix and task.schedule != null

    ensures: task.next_due = next_match(task.schedule, after: now)

    @guidance
        -- Decided with the user (task #98505, review 2026-10-23 C4): a phoenix
        -- run that fired, was abandoned and is moved back to backlog would
        -- otherwise sit there with a schedule and no due time, never firing
        -- and showing nothing wrong. Computed from now, so a slot that passed
        -- while the task was out of backlog is not fired late (S4).
        -- task.schedule_error is kept: only a launch or setting the schedule
        -- clears a recorded failure. A non-phoenix task is not re-armed; its
        -- run is spent.
}

rule ClosingCancelsThePendingRun {
    -- Every close, from any prior status: ExitSession (pr-workflow.allium),
    -- either action, RequestCloseTask (store-split.allium), and a hand status
    -- write into done (the TUI's moves and editor), which is a close too.
    when: TaskClosed(task)

    ensures: task.next_due = null

    @guidance
        -- A close of a task that was not in backlog is no move out of backlog,
        -- so LeavingBacklogCancelsThePendingRun does not reach it. Yet a task
        -- closed while a user-origin request still held its claim kept its
        -- due time (the claim keeps it until the launch ends), and a later
        -- move back to backlog would fire it at once. A closed one-off is
        -- spent. A phoenix task the close moves to done hands its schedule to
        -- the successor its respawn creates, armed there.
}

rule DispatchConsumesTheDueTime {
    when: request: store_split/DispatchRequest.state transitions_to dispatched

    let task = request.task

    ensures:
        if exists task:
            task.next_due = null
            task.schedule_error = null

    @guidance
        -- Every origin, not only schedule: a scheduled task launched early by
        -- hand has had its run, and a one-off must not fire again if it is
        -- later moved back to backlog. task.schedule is kept as a record; on a
        -- phoenix task it is what the successor inherits (PhoenixRespawn).
        -- A launch that fails does not reach this rule; see
        -- ScheduledLaunchFails.
}

rule ScheduledLaunchFails {
    when: request: store_split/DispatchRequest.state transitions_to failed

    requires: request.origin = schedule

    let task = request.task
    -- Only a failure that leaves the task in backlog: one that releases its
    -- claim (store-split.allium: ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest), or one whose
    -- task a hand move put back in backlog meanwhile. A task that moved on
    -- first is not a launch that left it in backlog: a close above all, since
    -- CloseFailsOpenRequests fails the request after the Done write.
    requires: exists task and
        (task.status = backlog or (task.status = running and task.worktree = null))

    ensures:
        if exists task and task.schedule != null:
            task.schedule_error = request.reason
            task.next_due =
                if task.phoenix: next_match(task.schedule, after: now) else: null

    @guidance
        -- S7. Applied in the same transaction as the failure and its claim
        -- release (store-split.allium: ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest), so the
        -- task returns to backlog already disarmed or re-armed: no board can
        -- see it back in backlog with the old, passed due time and fire it
        -- again. Every way a request fails while the task is claimed or back
        -- in backlog reaches it (a reported failure, the backstop timer, a
        -- host restart);
        -- a deleted task has nothing left to mark, and a closed one is Done,
        -- its pending run already cancelled (ClosingCancelsThePendingRun). A
        -- request of any other origin that fails leaves the schedule
        -- untouched: the person who launched it by hand sees the failure on
        -- the request, and the schedule still fires at its own time.
}

-- Two rules owned elsewhere carry the rest of this spec, written into them
-- rather than restated here:
--   - store-split.allium: RequestDispatch refuses a schedule-origin request
--     for a task that is not due by the store's clock, and refuses the epic
--     chain a task whose due time is still ahead (S8).
--   - tasks.allium: PhoenixRespawn gives the successor the predecessor's
--     schedule and a next_due computed from the moment of the respawn (S4,
--     S5). So "0 9 * * 1" on a run that finishes Wednesday next fires the
--     following Monday 09:00 UTC, and a run that took two weeks skips the
--     Monday it overran.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant DueTimeNeedsASchedule {
    for task in core/Tasks:
        task.next_due = null or task.schedule != null
}

invariant DueTimeIsAMatch {
    -- next_due is always a minute the stored cron matches.
    for task in core/Tasks:
        task.next_due = null or cron_matches(task.schedule, task.next_due)
}
